    "physical_description": "Sarong of waxed and dyed cotton batik. The field is patterned with stylised peacocks, plants, fish and other motifs, repeated on a background of undulating lines. The head (kepala) depicts conventional plant motifs. The cotton field is waxed with the tjanting and dyed indigo and brown on a white ground. The head and borders are waxed with copper blocks and dyed red with white reserved.",
    "summary": "The field of this cotton batik sarong from Cheribon in northern Java is patterned with stylized birds, plants, fish and other motifs, repeated on a background of undulating lines. The 'head' (kepala) depicts conventional plant motifs.\r\n\r\nBatik is the name given in Indonesia to the process of applying wax resist-dyed patterns to cloth. The origins of batik in Indonesia are still obscure, and it is possible that methods of wax resist have a long history in Indonesia, but the method using the tjanting - a small copper vessel with one or more spouts and a handle of reed or bamboo- to apply the wax to the cloth presupposes the availability of very finely woven cottons, which during the last 150 years have mainly been introduced from Europe and Japan. Cheribon is the second most important centre of batik production in Java after Central Java.",
